WEBVTT

1-1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.315
Your stack is awesome, but you need a way to manage it.

1-2
00:00:03.465 --> 00:00:10.463
StackOps is your OS-agnostic digital life manager. It covers 99% of your digital footprint.

1-3
00:00:10.613 --> 00:00:12.170
The applications you use.

1-4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.685
The way you configure them.

1-5
00:00:13.835 --> 00:00:15.670
Your secrets and credentials.

1-6
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.185
Your files and data.

1-7
00:00:17.335 --> 00:00:19.213
Your code and repositories.

1-8
00:00:19.363 --> 00:00:20.835
And the processes you run.

1-9
00:00:20.985 --> 00:00:22.649
All wrapped into one solution.

2-1
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.485
Start with a fresh, bare machine.

2-2
00:00:25.635 --> 00:00:29.091
Now bring back the environment you've spent ten years making your own.

2-3
00:00:29.241 --> 00:00:34.489
That's the goal: one command, five minutes, and your familiar digital life ready to use.

3-1
00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:42.258
Literally, you can install and configure your entire dev environment while casually inspecting a machine in the store.

3-2
00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:46.547
By the time they notice you are doing something unusual, the setup is done.

3-3
00:00:46.697 --> 00:00:51.454
This config is based on public setup, and ignores private setup and files.

4-1
00:00:52.483 --> 00:01:04.814
StackOps doesn't reinvent the wheel. It glues together the best open-source tools. It's a configurator, a package manager, a dotfile manager, a data sync solution, a digital life manager.

4-2
00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:10.511
Keep the stack you're comfortable with. StackOps manages the tools and settings that make it yours.

4-3
00:01:10.661 --> 00:01:16.229
It brings that approach across Linux, macOS, and Windows through a command-line interface.

5-1
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:23.650
One command hierarchy covers machine setup, cloud services, terminals, agents, and everyday tools.

5-2
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.856
DevOps brings together installation, configuration, networking, and security.

5-3
00:01:29.006 --> 00:01:35.449
Here, configuration brings back public and private settings, including dotfiles and terminal profiles.

5-4
00:01:35.599 --> 00:01:42.063
Alongside it, repository and data commands handle backup, synchronization, and retrieval of your work.

5-5
00:01:42.213 --> 00:01:48.570
Across the hierarchy, you can launch commands and workspaces, and orchestrate processes for everyday work.

6-1
00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:52.207
That's StackOps. Your digital life, sorted.

6-2
00:01:52.357 --> 00:01:56.816
Think of it as a Dockerfile: a recipe for how to reproduce your computer.
